"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[15946],{215946:e=>{e.exports="# How to navigate\n\n## The navigateWithinLakewatch Tool\n\nUse **`navigateWithinLakewatch`** to navigate the user. It takes:\n\n- **`thoughts`** — Short reasoning for the navigation.\n- **`intent`** — What the user wants to do (list, view, or create a given resource).\n- **`resourceType`** — The type of resource. One of: `datasource`, `rule`, `security-case`, `system-case`, `query`. Must match the intent (e.g. `rule` for view-rule, `security-case` for list-security-cases, `system-case` for list-system-cases).\n- **`resourceId`** — The resource ID; **required for all “view” intents**, `null` for list/create.\n- **`continueMessage`** — Optional message to send to the assistant on the destination page (e.g. “run the query”). Use when the task continues on that page. If the task depends on a previous resource, include its `resourceType` and `resourceId` so the destination agent can retrieve it with `apiGetLakewatchResourceById`. Leave `null` when only navigating to show a page.\n\n## Intent → resourceType → Route Mapping\n\nChoose the intent that matches the user’s goal, set **`resourceType`** to the corresponding resource type, then pass the correct `resourceId` (or `null`).\n\n| User goal                     | Intent                | resourceType    | resourceId   | Route                                                                                                           |\n| ----------------------------- | --------------------- | --------------- | ------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| List datasources              | `list-datasources`    | `datasource`    | `null`       | `/configure/datasources`                                                                                        |\n| Open a specific datasource    | `view-datasource`     | `datasource`    | datasourceId | `/configure/datasources/edit/{id}`                                                                              |\n| Create a new datasource       | `create-datasource`   | `datasource`    | `null`       | `/configure/datasources/create`                                                                                 |\n| List detection rules          | `list-rules`          | `rule`          | `null`       | `/configure/detection-rules`                                                                                    |\n| Open a specific rule          | `view-rule`           | `rule`          | ruleId       | `/configure/detection-rules/{id}`                                                                               |\n| Create a new rule             | `create-rule`         | `rule`          | `null`       | `/configure/detection-rules/add`                                                                                |\n| List security cases           | `list-security-cases` | `security-case` | `null`       | `/explore/security-cases`                                                                                       |\n| Open a specific security case | `view-security-case`  | `security-case` | caseId       | `/explore/security-cases/{id}`                                                                                  |\n| List system cases             | `list-system-cases`   | `system-case`   | `null`       | `/explore/system-cases`                                                                                         |\n| Open a specific system case   | `view-system-case`    | `system-case`   | caseId       | `/explore/system-cases/{id}`                                                                                    |\n| Open a specific query         | `view-query`          | `query`         | queryId      | `/explore/query/{id}`                                                                                           |\n| Create / go to query          | `create-query`        | `query`         | `null`       | Use **apiCreateLakewatchQuery** (title + initialSql), then navigate with `view-query` and the returned queryId. |\n\n## Rules for Choosing intent, resourceType, and resourceId\n\n1. **`resourceType`** — Always set to the resource type for the target: `datasource`, `rule`, `security-case`, `system-case`, or `query`. It must align with the intent (e.g. `view-rule` → `resourceType: 'rule'`, `list-security-cases` → `resourceType: 'security-case'`, `list-system-cases` → `resourceType: 'system-case'`).\n\n2. **“View” \"Edit\" or “open” a specific thing** — Use the matching `view-*` intent, set `resourceType` to that resource type, and set `resourceId` to the **ID of that resource** (datasource id, rule id, security case id, system case id, or query id). Never use a view intent without the corresponding `resourceId`. If you don’t have the ID yet, use **how-to-find-resource** first.\n\n3. **“List” or “show all”** — Use the matching `list-*` intent, set `resourceType` to that resource type, and set `resourceId` to `null`.\n\n4. **“Create” or “add new”** — For **queries**, use the **apiCreateLakewatchQuery** tool (title + initialSql) first; then optionally navigate with intent `view-query` and the returned `queryId`. For **datasources** or **rules**, first check the shared context: **can_create_datasource** (for create-datasource) and **can_create_rule** (for create-rule). If the value is false, do not navigate to the create page; instead explain that the user does not have permission and suggest contacting a workspace admin. If permitted, use the matching `create-*` intent, set `resourceType` to that resource type, and set `resourceId` to `null`.\n\n5. **Settings, observables, home, onboarding** — Not covered by the navigate tool’s intents. Use the closest list/view/create intent or tell the user to use the sidebar (e.g. “Open Settings from the Configure section in the sidebar”).\n\n6. **Ambiguous or missing ID** — If the user says “open the rule” but you don’t have a rule ID, use **how-to-find-resource** (list rules and match by name, or ask for ID) first, then navigate with the resolved `resourceId`.\n\n## continueMessage\n\nUse `continueMessage` when the user’s request implies an action **on the destination page** (e.g. “create a rule that does X” “go to case Y and add a comment”). If the task depends on content from a previous resource, include its `resourceType` and `resourceId` in the **continueMessage** as `fromResourceType` and `fromResourceId` so the destination agent can use `apiGetLakewatchResourceById`. Leave it `null` when you are only navigating to show a page.\n\n## Summary\n\n- **Navigation**: Use `navigateWithinLakewatch` with `thoughts`, `intent`, `resourceType` (`datasource` | `rule` | `security-case` | `system-case` | `query`), and `resourceId` (required for view-\\*).\n- **Create datasource/rule**: Check **can_create_datasource** or **can_create_rule** from shared context first; if false, explain lack of permission and do not navigate to the create page.\n- **Create query**: Use **apiCreateLakewatchQuery** (title + initialSql) to create a query; then use `navigateWithinLakewatch` with intent `view-query` and the returned queryId to open it.\n- **continueMessage**: Use for follow-up actions on the destination page; if the task depends on a previous resource, include its resourceType and resourceId so the destination can use `apiGetLakewatchResourceById`.\n\nResource definitions are in the base instructions. For finding a resource by ID or name before navigating, use **how-to-find-resource**.\n"}}]);
